Repair only the supplied scientific or grammatical issues. Return {sections:[{id:number,patches:[{before:string,after:string,issueIndex:number}],reason:string}]}. Include each supplied section exactly once. Use exact unique substrings in its candidate. At most 3 non-overlapping patches per section; their combined BEFORE text must cover at most 35% of that section. issueIndex refers to that section's issues. Keep wording elsewhere unchanged. Restore the original meaning, uncertainty and proposal status. No extra style pass. If bounded repairs cannot solve the issues, return patches:[] and explain.
INPUT DATA:
{"sections": [{"id": 2, "heading": "Related Work", "original": "The measurement brief specifies a threshold of 900 microsiemens per centimetre and defines an exceedance as a reading above that value [S1]. Equality is therefore not an exceedance. The experimental brief requires the threshold to remain fixed for both raw and corrected readings [S2]. This isolates the effect of correction from a change in alert policy.", "candidate": "The measurement brief sets a threshold of 900 microsiemens per centimetre, defining an exceedance as any reading above that value [S1]. Equality to the threshold does not constitute an exceedance. The experimental brief mandates that this threshold remain constant for both raw and drift-corrected readings [S2]. This design ensures that any changes in alert counts are attributable to the correction process rather than alterations in the alert policy.", "issues": [{"originalQuote": "This isolates the effect of correction from a change in alert policy.", "revisedQuote": "This design ensures that any changes in alert counts are attributable to the correction process rather than alterations in the alert policy.", "problem": "The revision strengthens a controlled comparison into a blanket causal guarantee. A fixed threshold rules out threshold changes as an explanation but does not establish that any change in alert counts must result from correction."}]}, {"id": 3, "heading": "Methodology", "original": "We will compare eight sensors over 21 days [S2]. Drift corrections will be estimated from the reference instrument and applied without consulting the resulting alert counts. The raw readings will remain available for inspection. We will record periods with missing reference measurements rather than invent corrections for intervals where the necessary evidence is unavailable.", "candidate": "We will compare eight sensors over 21 days [S2]. Drift corrections will be estimated from the reference instrument and applied without consulting the resulting alert counts. Raw readings will be retained for inspection, and periods with missing reference measurements will be recorded rather than inventing corrections for intervals where evidence is unavailable.", "issues": [{"originalQuote": "We will record periods with missing reference measurements rather than invent corrections for intervals where the necessary evidence is unavailable.", "revisedQuote": "periods with missing reference measurements will be recorded rather than inventing corrections for intervals where evidence is unavailable.", "problem": "The passive construction leaves 'inventing corrections' without a coherent grammatical actor. Preserve the explicit statement that the researchers will record missing-reference periods rather than invent corrections."}]}, {"id": 5, "heading": "Limitations and Conclusion", "original": "A reference instrument can itself have error, and the study covers only a limited observation period. Conductivity alerts cannot establish pathogen concentration or determine whether water is safe to drink [S1]. The proposed analysis will therefore stop at measurement agreement and alert behaviour. It will not provide drinking-water safety recommendations [S2].", "candidate": "A reference instrument can itself have errors, and the study covers only a limited observation period [S1]. Conductivity alerts cannot establish pathogen concentration or determine whether water is safe to drink [S1]. The proposed analysis will therefore focus on measurement agreement and alert behavior, without providing drinking-water safety recommendations [S2].", "issues": [{"originalQuote": "A reference instrument can itself have error, and the study covers only a limited observation period.", "revisedQuote": "A reference instrument can itself have errors, and the study covers only a limited observation period [S1].", "problem": "The added citation attributes reference-instrument error and the limited observation period to S1, which states neither."}, {"originalQuote": "The proposed analysis will therefore stop at measurement agreement and alert behaviour.", "revisedQuote": "The proposed analysis will therefore focus on measurement agreement and alert behavior", "problem": "'Focus on' weakens the explicit boundary that the analysis will stop at measurement agreement and alert behaviour."}]}], "sources": [{"id": "S1", "title": "Controlled source packet: domain and evidence brief", "excerpt": "The proposed study concerns conductivity alerts from river sensors. Conductivity is not a direct measurement of pathogen concentration. A reference instrument will be used to identify sensor drift. The alert threshold is 900 microsiemens per centimetre, and values equal to the threshold are not exceedances.", "content_kind": "author_constructed_evaluation_fixture"}, {"id": "S2", "title": "Controlled source packet: experimental design brief", "excerpt": "Eight sensors will be compared over 21 days. Raw readings and drift-corrected readings will use the same fixed threshold. Corrections will be estimated from the reference instrument, not from whether an alert appears plausible. The study will not make drinking-water safety recommendations.", "content_kind": "author_constructed_evaluation_fixture"}]}
